  {
   "id": "e2-102",
   "name": "Review: the Simple Present tense"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "The Simple Present is used for habits, facts, timetables and feelings:\n“The choir practises on Fridays.” “The sun rises in the east.” “The film starts at seven.” “I like makossa.”\nForm: the base verb; with he, she, it (or one person/thing) add -s or -es: plays, watches, goes, carries (y → ies after a consonant), has.\nNegative: do not (don't) / does not (doesn't) + base verb: “She doesn't sing.”\nQuestion: Do / Does + subject + base verb: “Does he play the guitar?”\nTime words: always, usually, often, sometimes, never, every day, on Mondays.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "v": "watch"
  },
  "problem": "Write “{v}” with “she” in the Simple Present, then make it negative.",
  "steps": [
   "With she, add -s or -es: she {thirdPerson(v)}.",
   "Negative: she doesn't + base verb: she doesn't {v}."
  ],
  "answer": "she {thirdPerson(v)}; she doesn't {v}",
  "checks": []
 },
